Scan to Folder 1101 error on Windows\n-\nDestination host does not exist on the network.: {class: note}
classes: {
    step: {shape: rectangle}
    diagnose: {shape: diamond}
    fixed: {shape: rectangle; style: {fill: "#90FF90"; stroke: "#00A000";}}
    failure: {shape: rectangle; style: {fill: "#FF9090"; stroke: "#A00000";}}
    note: {shape: page;}
}

#Check for Private Network
1: Verify the device is set to \n"Private Network" {class: step}

#Windows 11 
1-1: |md
1. Press Start Key
2. Select "Settings"
3. Select "Network & Internet"
4. Select either "Wi-Fi" or "Ethernet"
5. Under "Network profile type", select "Private network"
| {class: note}
1 -> 1-1: Windows 11

#Windows 10
1-2: |md
1. Press Start Key
2. Press "Settings"
3. Press "Network & Internet"
4. Select "Properties" button for either "Wi-Fi" or "Ethernet"
5. Select "Private" under "Network profile"
| {class: note}
1 -> 1-2: Windows 10

#Check for File and printer sharing
2: Verify "File and printer sharing" is enabled {class: step}
1 -> 2: Verified Private Network is selected

#Windows 11
2-1: |md
1. Press Start Key
2. Select "Settings"
3. Select "Network & Internet"
4. Select "Advanced network settings"
5. Select "Advanced sharing settings"
6. In the section for "Private networks", enable "File and printer sharing"
| {class: note}
2 -> 2-1: Windows 11

#Windows 10
2-2: |md
1. Press Start Key
2. Press "Settings"
3. Press "Network & Internet"  
4. Select "Network and Sharing Center"
5. Select "Change advanced sharing settings"
6. In the section for "Private networks", enable "file and printer sharing"
| {class: note}
2 -> 2-2: Windows 10

#Attempt to ping the device via hostname
3: Can you ping the target \nPC by its hostname?\n"ping (hostname)" {class: diagnose}
2 -> 3: Verified file and printer sharing is enabled

#---------------------------------
#-----CANNOT PING BY HOSTNAME-----
#---------------------------------

4: Can't ping PC by hostname
3 -> 4: No
4: {
    #---------------------------------------------
    #-----Test for Corrupt DNS resolver cache-----
    #---------------------------------------------
    4-1: |md
    1. On target PC open Command Prompt and run "ipconfig". Take note of the IPv4 Address shown
    2. Run "hostname". Take note of the hostname shown
    3. Run "ping (hostname) -4" Take note the IP address shown
    4. Does the IP Address shown in step 2 match the first IP Address Noted in step 1? 
    | {class: diagnose}

    #Purge DNS resolver cache and test again
    4-1-1: |md
    1. On target PC open Command Prompt and run "ipconfig /flushdns" 
    - If target goes offline, wait until network restarts 
    - If network never comes back online, reboot target PC.
    2. Repeat last diagnostic step. 
    3. Does "ipconfig" IP address now match IP address seen when using ping command?
    | {class: diagnose}
    4-1 -> 4-1-1: No

    #Router DNS Cache is corrupt
    4-1-1-1: The DNS cache is corrupt on the router. Altering router data to resolve the root\nof the issue is outside of our scope of work. Set the device to use the IP Address of the\ntarget and inform end user connection may fail in future when the target PC aquires a new IP Address\n unless root issue is resolved or if a IP Reservation is placed on Network {class: failure}
    4-1-1 -> 4-1-1-1: No

    #Clearing local DNS cache resolved issue
    4-1-1-2: DNS Resolver Cache was Corrupted on target PC. Cleaning the local DNS cache restored connection. {class: fixed}
    4-1-1 -> 4-1-1-2: Yes

    #----------------------------------------
    #-----Test for DNS failure on router-----
    #----------------------------------------
    4-1-2: On target PC open Command Prompt and run "ping (hostname).local"\nDoes pinging the hostname now work? {class: diagnose}
    4-1 -> 4-1-2: Yes

    #Unable to Resolve hostname
    4-1-2-1: Hostname remains unable to be resolved due to unidentifyable fault with either with customer network or the target PC.\nSet the device to use the IP Address of the target and inform end user connection may fail in future when the target PC aquires a new IP Address\n unless root issue is resolved or if a IP Reservation is placed on Network {class: failure}
    4-1-2 -> 4-1-2-1: No

    #DNS Failure
    4-1-2-2: Router is unable to resolve hostnames correctly. You are able to resolve the hostname by using mDNS resolution which is\na DNS broadcast that bypasses DNS. MFPs do not support mDNS so appending .local to the Hostname in the MFP will not\nresolve the issue. Altering router data to resolve the root of the issue is outside of our scope of work. {class: failure}
    4-1-2 -> 4-1-2-2: Yes
}

#----------------------------------
#-----ABLE TO PING BY HOSTNAME-----
#----------------------------------
5: Ping PC by hostname successful
3 -> 5: Yes
5: {
    #--------------------------------------
    #-----Test if Network Share Exists-----
    #--------------------------------------
    5-1: On target PC open Command Prompt and run "net share". Do you see a scan folder listed? {class: diagnose}
    
    #Scan folder isnt share or doesnt exist
    5-1-1: No Scan folder exists on PC. Create and share out a scan folder. {class: fixed}
    5-1 -> 5-1-1: No 

    #Create Scan Folder
    5-1-1-1: |md
    1. Open File Explorer
    2. Right click on folder and click "Properties"
    3. Click "Sharing" tab and Click "Advanced Sharing"
    4. Check "Share this folder"
    5. Click "Permissions"
    6. Click "Add"
    7. In the text box input the username for the network share
    8. Press "Check Names"
    9. Press "OK"
    10. Check "Full Control" underneath "Allow"
    11. Click "Apply"
    12. Click "OK"
    13. Click "Apply"
    14. Click "OK"
    15. Click "Share"
    16. Click the dropdown and select the same user as step 7
    17. If user is not "Owner" Select "Read/Write" as Permission Level
    18. Click "Share"
    19. Click "Done"
    20. Click "Close"
    | {class: note}
    5-1-1 -> 5-1-1-1: Create a folder for Scanning

    #----------------------------
    #-----Test Network Share-----
    #----------------------------
    5-1-2: Attempt to connect to Network Share off another PC.\nAre you able to both read and write to the scan folder? {class: diagnose}
    5-1 -> 5-1-2: Yes

    #How to connect off another PC
    5-1-2-1: |md
    1. Open File Explorer
    2. Right click in left and and select "Map network drive"
    3. In the folder box put "\\(hostname of target PC)\(name of shared folder)"
    - example- \\LAPTOP-A1B2C3\Scans
    4. Check "Connect using different credentials"
    5. Click "Finish"
    6. Input the username and password of the account that has been given Read/Write Permission for the shared out folder on the target PC
    7. Click "OK"
    | {class: note}
    5-1-2 -> 5-1-2-1: How to connect to a shared folder from another PC

    #SMB blocked by target PC
    5-1-2-2: Target PC either has issue outside our scope of work to resolve or has SMB blocked by Firewall. Report findings to end user and discuss alternatives {class: failure}
    5-1-2 -> 5-1-2-2: No

    #Check for Legacy SMB model
    5-1-2 -> See list of\nsupported models: Yes {class: step; style: {stroke-dash:5}}

    #-----------------------------
    #-----Test Different Port-----
    #-----------------------------

    5-1-3: If the port number on the destination is set to 445, set port to 139.\nIf the port number is set to 139, set port to 445. Does SMB work now? {class: diagnose}
    5-1-2 -> 5-1-3: Yes

    #Wrong Port
    5-1-3-1: Target PC does not listen to either port 139 or 445.\nUsing the alternate port resolved the issue {class: fixed}
    5-1-3 -> 5-1-3-1: Yes
}
#---------------------------
#-----LESS COMMON FIXES-----
#---------------------------

6: Less Common Fixes
5 -> 6
6: {
    #Initialize Device and try again
    6-1: Make a full device backup and then initialize the unit.\nUpon reconnecting to network and reprogramming scan destination,\nattempt to send another scan. Does SMB work now? {class: diagnose}

    #Initializing resolved issue
    6-1-1: Unidentified bug in unit that was resolved with initialization {class: fixed}
    6-1 -> 6-1-1: Yes

    #Initializing did not resolve issue
    6-1-2: Unable to resolve source of issue. Consult with end user about alternatives {class: failure}
    6-1 -> 6-1-2: No
}

#Check for legacy SMB device
7: |md
Is the device one of the following models? 
- TASKalfa 3050ci/3550ci/4550ci/5550ci
- TASKalfa 1800
- TASKalfa 2200
- FS-1220MFP/1320MFP/1325MFP
- FS-6025MFP/6030MFP
- FS-6525MFP/6530MFP
- FS-C8020MFP/C8025MFP
- FS-C8520MFP/C8525MFP
| {class: step}

#Legacy SMB not supported
7-1: Legacy SMB (SMB 1.0) is not Supported on this model Kyocera.\nInform End User this is disabled by default due to a known Security Exploit (WannaCry).\nInform end user this legacy service can be enabled to allow scanning, but we are not liable if issues occur as a result. {class: failure}
7 -> 7-1: Yes

#Enable Legacy SMB
7-2: Search for "Turn Windows Features on or off" Scroll Down and open the dropdown for "SMB 1.0/CIFS File Sharing Support"\nCheck both "SMB1.0/CIFS Client" and "SMB1.0/CIFS Server". Uncheck "SMB1.0/CIFS Automatic Removal". Apply the setting and restart if necessary. {class: fixed}
7-1 -> 7-2: Enable Legacy SMB (SMB 1.0)
